                        "username": "LeoWu-wiwynn"
                    },
                    "message": "Summary of my intent\n- Record NIC power state events in the NIC card power-cycle script for OpenBMC Yosemite4.\n\n\nChanges in this version\n\nNamespace:\n- Moved to State/InventoryItemPower.events.yaml\n\nEvents renamed:\n- InventoryItemPowerGood / InventoryItemPowerLost\n(emphasizes the runtime aggregated \"power-good\" condition)\n\nEvents consumers:\n- Platform scripts in my case, (e.g., Yosemite4 NIC power-cycle: https://github.com/openbmc/openbmc/blob/master/meta-facebook/meta-yosemite4/recipes-phosphor/pldm/files/nic-powercycle).\n- The script will validate the aggregated rail status (via CPLD bits for 12V and 3.3V) and emit InventoryItemPowerGood or InventoryItemPowerLost.\n\nSemantics clarified:\n- \"Power-good\" means all platform-defined operational (non-standby) rails for the item are asserted and stable.\n- This is distinct from State/Power fault or recovery events (those represent errors).\n- No automatic child FRU cascade for the NIC card power-cycle in this case.\n\n\nIs script-based log events after hardware validation the acceptable pattern?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
                    "line": 0,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "LeoWu-wiwynn",
                        "email": "leo.wu.wiwynn@gmail.com",
                        "username": "LeoWu-wiwynn"
                    },
                    "message": "Hi @patrick@stwcx.xyz\n\nI've updated the code based on your feedback.\n- Moved the events from \u2018Inventory\u2019 to \u2018State\u2019.\n- To avoid confusion with the existing State/Power.events.yaml (which related to  power rail), I added a new file to represent inventory power good/lost events instead of \"power on\" for clearer semantics.\nCould you please review the changes and provide any further suggestions? \nThanks."
                },
